    "description": "Iranian society is shaped by changing population patterns, urban settlement, family relationships, migration, and unequal access to work and services. Understanding these changes requires attention to differences between generations, places, and residents with different legal and economic circumstances.",
    "tags": ["Iranian society", "Population", "Social change", "Migration", "Urbanization", "Welfare"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:52:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:52:47Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Society, Population and Social Change | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore how demography, cities, migration, family life, employment and unequal access to services shape social change in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading society through connected changes",
        "content": "Iran's social transformation cannot be reduced to a single national average. Population ageing, changing households, urban settlement, and access to employment operate together, but their consequences vary between communities. The World Bank's historical poverty diagnostic connects these developments while documenting differences in welfare between households and regions. Its findings describe the period studied rather than a timeless portrait of Iranian life. [s1]\n\nThis directory approaches society through seven connected subjects: demography, migration, urbanization, family relationships, employment and welfare, social challenges, and regional communities. These branches answer different questions. Demography examines population composition; migration examines movement; welfare research asks how people obtain resources and protection. A change in one does not establish an equivalent improvement in another.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "settlement-and-place",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An urban majority with substantial regional differences",
        "content": "The selected results of Iran's 2016 Population and Housing Census show why place matters. Table 5 reports a national urbanization rate of 74%, compared with 94% in Tehran province and 48% in Sistan and Baluchestan. These are provincial shares of residents classified as urban, not the populations of the cities of Tehran or Zahedan. Nor does urban classification measure the quality of housing, public transport, or employment. [s2]\n\nA national urban majority therefore leaves considerable variation to explain. Regional and community studies help connect settlement patterns to local circumstances. Readers should distinguish a province, a city within it, and the surrounding metropolitan area: their boundaries and populations answer different questions. The census publication itself separates urban, rural, and unsettled populations, preserving distinctions that disappear when all residents are described simply as city dwellers. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "families-and-generations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Families and the movement of generations",
        "content": "Households connect population change with everyday responsibilities. The World Bank's diagnostic describes smaller households alongside urbanization and an older population. A household, however, is a statistical unit rather than a complete map of kinship. Its size alone cannot tell us who provides care, how relatives share money, or whether family members live nearby. Those questions require evidence about relationships and arrangements, not just a headcount. [s1]\n\nAgeing also concerns people well before old age. UNFPA, the United Nations Population Fund, advocates a life-course approach in Iran: health, participation, accessibility, and support should be considered across successive stages of life. Its ageing programme identifies gaps in welfare and social systems as research priorities. This approach treats older people as members of communities with different capabilities and circumstances, rather than assuming that everyone beyond an age threshold has identical needs. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "migration-and-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Migration, documentation and access",
        "content": "Migration includes moves within Iran as well as movement across its borders. International displacement adds a distinct legal dimension. UNHCR's 2025 annual results report distinguishes recognized refugees from other forcibly displaced residents with different documentation. It describes both access to public services and continuing protection concerns, including the consequences of documentation changes and return procedures. Its account supports neither an assumption of universal exclusion nor an assumption that formal inclusion removes practical barriers. [s4]\n\nEducation illustrates the difference between eligibility and effective access. UNICEF identifies overcrowding, limited school space, economic and geographical obstacles, transport difficulties, and gender disparities among the barriers affecting children in Iran. Its programme describes intended responses; those objectives should not be mistaken for independently demonstrated nationwide results. The useful question is whether a child can attend and learn consistently, alongside the formal question of admission. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "work-and-welfare",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Employment is one part of social security",
        "content": "Employment research must distinguish unemployment from participation. Someone outside the labour force is not counted as unemployed merely because they have no job. The World Bank's historical diagnostic describes how withdrawal from job-seeking complicated interpretation of labour-market conditions during the COVID-19 crisis. A falling unemployment rate, considered alone, consequently cannot establish that more people obtained paid work. [s1]\n\nWelfare analysis also needs to distinguish a statistical poverty threshold from wider experiences of insecurity. The World Bank's Poverty and Inequality Platform labels its international thresholds, purchasing-power basis, and reference years explicitly. These are essential qualifications: a poverty estimate for an earlier survey year does not measure conditions on the day a webpage is accessed, and an international threshold is not automatically Iran's national poverty line. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "using-the-directory",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following the evidence",
        "content": "A useful reading of social change moves between national patterns and local experience. The demography branch explains population measures; migration and urbanization examine movement and settlement; family and community studies investigate relationships that aggregate statistics cannot fully describe. Employment, welfare, and social-challenge pages then examine how resources and opportunities are distributed.\n\nEvidence quality remains part of the subject. UNFPA identifies opportunities to improve integration of Iranian statistical sources and harmonization of civil registration with other data. Readers should therefore check the reference period, population covered, and method before treating apparently conflicting figures as contradictory. A census count, a household survey result, and a modelled population estimate can all be useful while measuring different things. [s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s7"]
      }
    ],
