  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-10-1",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/demography/",
    "name": "Demography",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Demography examines the size, composition and distribution of Iran's population through births, deaths, migration and the passage of generations. Census results, registration records and population estimates provide complementary evidence, but their definitions and reference periods must remain distinct.",
    "tags": ["Demography", "Fertility", "Mortality", "Population ageing", "Census", "Population distribution"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:52:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:52:47Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Demography of Iran: Population, Fertility and Ageing | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's demographic transition and learn how census counts, fertility, life expectancy, age structure and migration fit together."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "population-and-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Population is a stock; demographic events are flows",
        "content": "Demography studies how populations change and how their members are distributed by characteristics such as age and place of residence. Births add people, deaths remove them, and migration changes where people live. Age structure records the cumulative consequences of these processes as successive generations grow older. Research on Iran's demographic transition therefore examines fertility, mortality, and migration together. [s1]\n\nA population total is not an annual number of births, and a movement across a provincial boundary is not necessarily international migration. These distinctions matter when reading Iranian statistics: a national population can change while particular provinces follow different trajectories. The relevant denominator and geographical boundary belong beside the result, rather than being left for readers to infer. [s1]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fertility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What fertility measures",
        "content": "Total fertility rate summarizes a year's age-specific childbearing rates as births per woman. It describes a hypothetical lifetime under those rates, rather than counting the children already born to the average woman. It must also be distinguished from the number of births registered during the year. The latter depends on how many women are at childbearing ages as well as their childbearing rates. [s2]\n\nThis helps explain population momentum: earlier growth can leave numerous people entering childbearing ages, sustaining births even after fertility declines. The United Nations identifies momentum from past growth as a major demographic mechanism internationally. Applying that principle does not establish an exact date when Iran's population will peak; such a date requires a specified projection and its assumptions. [s3]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mortality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Life expectancy is a period measure",
        "content": "Life expectancy at birth summarizes the mortality conditions prevailing across age groups in a particular period. It asks how long a newborn would live if those death rates continued. It is neither a prediction of an individual child's lifespan nor the average age of people who died that year. [s4]\n\nThe distinction is especially useful when examining sudden mortality changes. A period indicator can worsen during a crisis without implying that everyone permanently loses the same number of years of life. Conversely, an improvement in the national measure does not demonstrate that all places or social groups experience identical survival conditions. The underlying age-specific rates and population coverage remain essential to interpretation. [s4]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transition-and-ageing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From demographic transition to an older population",
        "content": "UNFPA describes Iran as having reached low fertility and mortality and identifies population ageing as a major planning issue. Ageing concerns the changing balance of age groups, rather than a claim that children or young adults have disappeared. Its implications include the accessibility of services and the ability of welfare systems to respond to different needs across life. [s5]\n\nA large population at conventional working ages can create an economic opportunity, but it does not itself produce employment. Stanford's Iran 2040 research stresses the importance of jobs, skills, and economic conditions in converting demographic circumstances into benefits. Its projections belong to their publication vintage; they should not be presented as newly verified forecasts for October 2026. [s1]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "population-accounting",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How demographic change fits together",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Population at the beginning of a period\"] -->|\"Starting stock\"| E[\"Population at the end of the period\"]\n  B[\"Births during the period\"] -->|\"Add residents\"| E\n  C[\"Deaths during the period\"] -->|\"Subtract residents\"| E\n  D[\"Immigration minus emigration\"] -->|\"Add net migration\"| E\n  E -->|\"Classify residents by age\"| F[\"Population age structure\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This is a conceptual population-accounting diagram. It distinguishes the population stock from demographic flows; it does not estimate their size or imply that statistical revisions never occur.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-iran",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Counts, estimates and the limits of comparison",
        "content": "Iran's census summaries provide dated snapshots, including age and settlement information. International annual estimates answer a different need: they provide population series between enumerations using demographic evidence and methods. UNFPA identifies harmonization of civil registration and other statistical sources as an area for improvement. A difference between published totals therefore needs investigation before being interpreted as an error. [s6]\n\nThe pages below separate age structure, fertility, life expectancy, ageing, provincial distribution, and household change. Together they provide a framework for reading demographic claims without confusing a modelled estimate with a census count or a future scenario with an observed outcome.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      }
    ],
