    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's population age structure describes how residents are distributed across age groups. Annual estimates show that the share aged under fifteen rose slightly during the mid-2010s before declining, while broader age categories require careful interpretation when discussing employment and dependency.",
    "tags": ["Age structure", "Children", "Population estimates", "Dependency ratio", "Demographic transition"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:52:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:52:47Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Population Age Structure and Child Population Share | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Examine Iran's annual child-population share, understand age groups and distinguish demographic dependency from economic participation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-age-groups",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an age structure describes",
        "content": "Population age structure shows the distribution of residents across ages, rather than simply how many people live in a country. Common broad categories distinguish children, conventional working ages, and older residents. These boundaries make comparisons possible, but they are analytical conventions: they do not establish whether a particular person attends school, works, receives a pension, or provides care. [s1]\n\nThe World Bank's age-dependency definition is particularly explicit about this limitation. It compares residents below fifteen and above sixty-four with those aged fifteen to sixty-four. The resulting ratio describes age composition, not actual economic dependency. Some people outside the conventional working-age band work, while some inside it do not. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "annual-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A recent pattern with a modest reversal",
        "content": "The annual series below measures the percentage of Iran's population aged zero through fourteen. It is not a birth rate or an absolute count of children. The observations show a small increase during the middle of the displayed period followed by a decline. Thus, describing every recent year as a continuous fall would obscure the actual pattern. [s2]\n\nA falling share does not, by itself, establish that the number of children fell: the total population is the denominator. It also cannot isolate the contribution of births, survival, or migration. The indicator is useful for describing composition, while explaining its causes requires additional demographic evidence. [s2, s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "child-share-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: population aged 0–14 as a share of total population, 2012–2026",
          "dataset_id": "child-population-share",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["ages-0-14-share"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Percent of total population, 0–100 scale",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The displayed share rises modestly into 2018 and then declines through 2025. The final year has no inspected observation and remains a gap; the published values are demographic estimates rather than annual census counts. [s2]"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "child-share-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Published annual child-population shares and the unfilled 2026 observation",
          "dataset_id": "child-population-share"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparison-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping age thresholds and sources consistent",
        "content": "A discussion of older residents must retain its age threshold. A measure beginning at sixty includes people excluded from a measure beginning at sixty-five; the two cannot be exchanged without changing the population being described. Similarly, a share of the national population cannot be compared directly with a dependency ratio whose denominator is only the conventional working-age population. [s1]\n\nThe World Bank identifies the United Nations Population Division's World Population Prospects as the source for its child-share indicator. The annual values inspected here were distributed through FRED, the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis's economic database. Their many decimal places should not be read as evidence of equally precise enumeration: these are published demographic series, subject to their source methods and later revisions. [s2, s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "practical-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What this measure can support",
        "content": "Age composition helps frame questions about infrastructure and service needs. It does not determine the answer: the same population share can correspond to different absolute populations, settlement patterns, and participation rates. The World Bank's metadata specifically distinguishes demographic dependency from economic dependency and notes the planning relevance of age structure. [s1]\n\nThe child-share series is therefore a starting point. A fuller assessment should add compatible counts and other age groups from the same statistical vintage, then examine regional differences. This page does not infer the older-population share by subtracting the child share alone, or turn an age category into an employment estimate.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
      {
        "id": "child-population-share",
        "title": "Population ages 0–14, percent of total population",
